  "ideaStickerEffectTitle": "Sticker Effect",
  "ideaStickerEffectSummary": "Make subjects look like cut-out stickers using a white halo and black outer outline.",
  "ideaStickerEffectOverview": "Technique-first challenge. The illusion comes from edge treatment, not from a specific palette.",
  "ideaStickerEffectStep1": "Color the main subject normally first.",
  "ideaStickerEffectStep2": "Add a clean white halo around the full silhouette.",
  "ideaStickerEffectStep3": "Trace the outside edge of that white halo with a black line.",
  "ideaStickerEffectStep4": "Optional: add a tiny drop shadow under one side to make it pop.",
  "ideaStickerEffectEmbellishment1": "Keep halo width consistent for a cleaner cut-out look.",
  "ideaStickerEffectEmbellishment2": "If edges feel noisy, smooth with white pen before black contour.",
  "ideaPolaroidFrameTitle": "Polaroid Frame",
  "ideaPolaroidFrameSummary": "Draw a Polaroid frame, color only inside it, and keep everything outside in grayscale.",
  "ideaPolaroidFrameOverview": "Build a fake instant-photo composition. The interior gets full color while the rest stays in controlled grayscale.",
  "ideaPolaroidFrameStep1": "Sketch a Polaroid frame with a wider bottom border.",
  "ideaPolaroidFrameStep2": "Block everything outside the frame using 2-3 grayscale values.",
  "ideaPolaroidFrameStep3": "Color only the interior photo window with your subject colors.",
  "ideaPolaroidFrameStep4": "Add a subtle frame shadow so the photo appears on top of the page.",
  "ideaPolaroidFrameEmbellishment1": "Write a tiny date caption on the bottom frame for realism.",
  "ideaPolaroidFrameEmbellishment2": "Make outside grayscale slightly cooler to boost interior warmth.",
  "ideaSpotlightTitle": "Spotlight",
  "ideaSpotlightSummary": "Create a strong focal light with dark edges and smooth value falloff.",
  "ideaSpotlightOverview": "Direct attention with value contrast: bright center, dark perimeter, and clear light falloff.",
  "ideaSpotlightStep1": "Mark the spotlight center before laying color.",
  "ideaSpotlightStep2": "Keep the focal zone bright and high-contrast.",
  "ideaSpotlightStep3": "Darken values gradually as you move away from the center.",
  "ideaSpotlightStep4": "Finish with a soft vignette around page edges.",
  "ideaSpotlightEmbellishment1": "Use harder edges near the focal point, softer edges far away.",
  "ideaSpotlightEmbellishment2": "Avoid bright accents in corners so focus stays centered.",
  "ideaOneColorTitle": "One Color",
  "ideaOneColorSummary": "Use a single hue family and control contrast only through value and saturation.",
  "ideaOneColorOverview": "One hue, many values. Depth comes from shifts in lightness and saturation, not hue variety.",
  "ideaOneColorStep1": "Choose one dominant hue family for the full piece.",
  "ideaOneColorStep2": "Separate planes with value shifts, not hue shifts.",
  "ideaOneColorStep3": "Reserve highest contrast for your focal element.",
  "ideaOneColorStep4": "Use saturation variation to avoid flat areas.",
  "ideaOneColorEmbellishment1": "Use texture shifts to separate materials when hue variety is limited.",
  "ideaOneColorEmbellishment2": "Keep one micro-accent at the brightest value for focal clarity.",
  "ideaTwoColorsTitle": "Two Colors",
  "ideaTwoColorsSummary": "Build the scene using only two dominant hue families plus subtle support tones.",
  "ideaTwoColorsOverview": "Restrict the scene to two hue families and let contrast come from value, not palette breadth.",
  "ideaTwoColorsStep1": "Assign each major area to one of the two hue families.",
  "ideaTwoColorsStep2": "Keep transitions clean to preserve the two-tone concept.",
  "ideaTwoColorsStep3": "Use one neutral bridge only where needed.",
  "ideaTwoColorsStep4": "Keep one hue dominant and one supporting.",
  "ideaTwoColorsEmbellishment1": "Let one hue occupy larger shapes and the second hue act as accent.",
  "ideaTwoColorsEmbellishment2": "If harmony breaks, add a low-saturation bridge tone in transitions.",
  "ideaFiftyFiftyTitle": "50/50 Split",
  "ideaFiftyFiftySummary": "Split the composition in two moods. One side warm, the other cool, with a bridge color.",
  "ideaFiftyFiftyOverview": "Compose two distinct moods in one image. Keep each side coherent and blend with one bridge tone.",
  "ideaFiftyFiftyStep1": "Split the page vertically or horizontally.",
  "ideaFiftyFiftyStep2": "Assign warm hues to one side and cool hues to the other.",
  "ideaFiftyFiftyStep3": "Use one bridge tone at the boundary so the split feels intentional.",
  "ideaFiftyFiftyStep4": "Keep each half internally consistent in value and saturation.",
  "ideaFiftyFiftyEmbellishment1": "Mirror one small motif on both halves to tie the concept together.",
  "ideaFiftyFiftyEmbellishment2": "Keep one side slightly darker to avoid visual symmetry fatigue.",
  "ideaWarmColorsTitle": "Warm Colors",
  "ideaWarmColorsSummary": "Use only warm hues for an energetic, cozy, sunlit scene.",
  "ideaWarmColorsOverview": "Warm hues carry energy and intimacy. Keep shadows warm instead of neutral gray.",
  "ideaCoolColorsTitle": "Cool Colors",
  "ideaCoolColorsSummary": "Use cool hues for calm, depth, and atmospheric distance.",
  "ideaCoolColorsOverview": "Cool hues create depth and calm. Control value steps to avoid a flat finish.",
  "ideaPastelTitle": "Pastel",
  "ideaPastelSummary": "Light value and low saturation for a soft, dreamy finish.",
  "ideaPastelOverview": "Pastel scenes rely on value control and subtle contrast more than hue intensity.",
  "ideaNeonTitle": "Neon",
  "ideaNeonSummary": "High-energy, vivid colors that pop against dark support tones.",
  "ideaNeonOverview": "Neon needs contrast. Reserve at least one dark support area so bright accents can glow.",
  "ideaRainbowTitle": "Rainbow",
  "ideaRainbowSummary": "Distribute hue families across the artwork while keeping values controlled.",
  "ideaRainbowOverview": "Use broad hue coverage but keep one dominant hue family so the piece still feels unified.",
  "ideaUnlikelyColorsTitle": "Unlikely Colors",
  "ideaUnlikelyColorsSummary": "Try unexpected color pairings to make ordinary subjects feel stylized.",
  "ideaUnlikelyColorsOverview": "Unexpected combinations can feel intentional when value hierarchy stays clear.",
  "ideaRandomColorsTitle": "Random Colors",
  "ideaRandomColorsSummary": "Use a randomized palette and force cohesion through value planning.",
  "ideaRandomColorsOverview": "Random palettes look coherent if you keep one focal area and controlled contrast.",
  "ideaGrayscaleTitle": "Grayscale",
